    SPInfraEstateAppDeploymentTypeSchema = external_exports.enum(["API", "WEB", "PROCESSOR"]);
    SPInfraEstateAppGrantTypeSchema = external_exports.enum(["SEAL", "EDGE"]).describe("The cloud services an application's runtime may call AS ITSELF, with no credential. A database, a cache and a queue are reached with a user and a password, which arrive as settings. These are reached another way: the blueprint grants the application's role the right, and the workload acts as itself. A row lists the ones it claims, and a workload that did not claim one holds no grant for it \u2014 so a permission problem surfaces because a declaration was missing rather than because a role was too narrow to guess. A CLOSED list, so a manifest that names anything else is refused, and a new service is one new value. A grant is not restricted by `space`: an application that lists one is granted it, on the platform or in a space.");
    SPInfraEstateAppFramedByTypeSchema = external_exports.enum(["NONE", "PLATFORM"]).describe("Which pages may frame an application (RULE.SUPPORT.INFRA.121). A page's own policy cannot carry `frame-ancestors`, so the answer is a response header, sent by the web edge or the private pod from this fact in the app's row. A CLOSED list. `NONE` answers `frame-ancestors 'none'`; `PLATFORM` answers the platform's own pages, which are every surface of the platform and so same-site with the auth host.");
    SPInfraEstateExposeTypeSchema = external_exports.enum(["PUBLIC", "PRIVATE", "INTERNAL"]).describe("What a deployment's inbound surface IS \u2014 the one declaration driving which ingress, which zone, and whether it stands on the public attack surface at all. A LADDER, most open first. `PUBLIC` answers on the internet; `PRIVATE` answers on the internal zone, reachable by an operator arriving through the platform's own ranges; `INTERNAL` answers inside the cluster only and stands no ingress at all. **DECLARED, and the namespace is its ceiling.** Derived from `(mtype, ns)`, a private web app could not be expressed at all \u2014 there would be no way to say it. The rule, per DEC.SUPPORT.INFRA.027 as amended: exposure is declared, and the namespace bounds it. A `plt` or `vnd` deployment claiming `PUBLIC` is refused at resolve, by name, before any plan. **`NONE` is not a rung.** Resolve refuses a declaration carrying it, because a stale `NONE` would otherwise read as a missing field rather than as the wrong rung. An ENUM rather than a string, because the value crosses three boundaries: it is declared here, it is handed to a layer whose variable validates it, and it is asserted against in a `check`. A bare string type-checks at none of them, so a typo reaches tofu and comes back as a validation error naming the variable rather than the line that produced it.");
    SPInfraEstateHostingTypeSchema = external_exports.enum(["MANAGED", "CLUSTER"]).describe("Which rendering family serves an engine \u2014 the provider's own service, or the same engine as a workload beside the apps. Independent of size.");
    SPInfraEstateDataRoleTypeSchema = external_exports.enum(["ADM", "MIG", "RW", "RO"]).describe("The four data purposes. Grants are fixed per purpose, and which one a run holds is its run mode.");
